    date "2017-02-23T00:51:51Z"
    title "&#34;Does Cardio Burn Muscle And Kill Gains?&#34;"
    description "&#9658; THE BODY TRANSFORMATION BLUEPRINT&#10;Science-based muscle building and fat loss system:&#10;http://www.BodyTransformationTruth.com&#10;&#10;&#9658; REALSCIENCE ATHLETICS&#10;No B.S, premium quality supplements you can trust:&#10;http://www.RealScienceAthletics.com&#10;&#10;CONNECT WITH ME&#10;Blog: http://www.SeanNal.com&#10;Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/Sean_Nalewanyj/&#10;Facebook: https://www.facebook.com/SeanNalewanyjOfficial&#10;&#10;GET YOUR FREE CUSTOM MEAL PLAN&#10;http://www.SeanNal.com/free-meal-plan.php&#10;&#10;TAKE MY ONLINE FITNESS QUIZ&#10;http://www.SeanNal.com/quiz-questions.php&#10;&#10;--------------------------------------------------------------------&#10;&#10;Video Summary:&#10;&#34;Does Cardio Burn Muscle And Kill Gains?&#34;&#10;http://www.SeanNal.com/articles/training/does-cardio-burn-muscle.php&#10;&#10;When it comes to the subject of cardio, many out there have taken an &#34;anti-cardio&#34; stance by saying that it interferes with weight training adaptations and has no benefit whatsoever beyond weight trainnig.&#10;&#10;However, it's really not a black and white issue of cardio while bulking being either &#34;good or bad&#34; or that you should either perform tons of cardio or none at all.&#10;&#10;Instead, the answer lies on a bell curve where performing some cardio is totally fine and will deliver certain benefits, but where going overboard certainly can have a negative impact on your muscle gains.&#10;&#10;People often ask me, &#34;does cardio burn muscle&#34;, and the answer is that this will only be a concern if you perform too much.&#10;&#10;Excessive cardio places an additional stressor onto the body during the week that it must divert time and resources to recover from (both in terms of muscle damage and CNS stress), and it also burns additional calories which may become a problem if you're not properly compensating by increasing your caloric intake in order to maintain a surplus for muscle growth.&#10;&#10;Not only that, but performing cardio in close proximity to your workouts could also affect your actual weight training performance. For example, performing HIIT sprints before a leg workout or an exhaustive rowing session before a back workout.&#10;&#10;How much cardio is too much?&#10;&#10;I would say that during a bulking phase, 2-3 cardio sessions per week is totally fine and not an issue for concern as long as you space it out intelligently during the week, and most people could probably get away with 4 if they really wanted to, though I'd consider that the upper maximum.&#10;&#10;Also make sure to perform a mix of both high intensity cardio and low intensity cardio, as the HIIT variety is much more stressful to the body and thus the frequency should be moderated.&#10;&#10;Not only is performing some cardio while bulking &#34;fine&#34;, but it also has certain benefits as well since low intensity cardio can actually improve recovery in between workouts, and cardio in general also improves insulin sensitiviy and sleep quality.&#10;&#10;Beyond body composition, cardio itself is also something that is just good for your physical and mental health, period.&#10;&#10;It improves mental functioning by increasing levels of BDNF (a protein that assists in the growth of neurons), increases serotonin/norepinephrine levels (thereby reducing depression and anxiety), reduces the risk of certain diseases and of course improves basic cardiovascular conditioning.&#10;&#10;So, if you live a relatively sedentary lifestyle aside from weight training, I would highly recommend getting in a couple cardio sessions per week just for those benefits alone.&#10;&#10;Bottom line:&#10;&#10;Does cardio burn muscle? Does cardio kill gains? Absolutely not, as long as you're doing it in moderation (2-3 sessions per week is the sweet spot) and your nutrition is on point.  And even if you're not using it as a tool for building muscle or losing fat more effectively, your overall quality of life in general will almost certainly be improved by including some cardio throughout the week.&#10;&#10;P.S. If you found these bodybuilding tips helpful, make sure to get your personalized training, nutrition and supplement plans using my interactive video presentation below:&#10;&#10;http://www.BodyTransformationTruth.com"
    channel_title "Sean Nalewanyj"
    channel_id "UCGXRegKvroyGv8HbN35AjKA"
