  node [
    id 4714
    label "ub98tgcVAZM"
    step 1
    date "2018-10-17T02:50:14Z"
    title "VShred | 4 Things YOU Should Know Before Working Out"
    description "For a workout and nutrition program tailored to your body type, take my free body type quiz and get the right plan personalized for your goals - http://vshred.fit/4WishIKnew&#10;&#10;My Favorite Fat Burner - http://vshred.fit/YTBurn&#10;&#10;What's going on guys! In todays video I'm going to go over 4 of the top things I wish I knew before I got started on my fitness journey. Hopefully some of these help you to cut your learning curve, and get better faster results on your journey!&#10;&#10;First, is it doesn't matter how much weight you lift. One of the biggest pet peeves I have is when people ask me how much I bench, squat, deadlift, or brags because they can lift heavy weight. But, none of that really matters unless you're doing a powerlifting competition. If you're just training for health, or to look better - what's really important is the contraction you're getting on the exercise.&#10;&#10;Second, diet is king. It doesn't matter how much you workout if your diet is garbage. You cannot out train a bad diet. You can train hard and often but if you eat like crap - you'll never see the results you really want. If you're trying to lose weight, you need to be in a caloric deficit. period. If you're trying to gain weight, you need to be in a caloric surplus, period. If you're not sure on how many calories you should be consuming, check out the link above to take my free body type quiz and find yours out today!&#10;&#10;Third, supplements CAN help. No matter what you've been told or the industry tells you - the bottom line is that supplements are indeed a supplement, meaning they make up where your nutrition is lacking. You have to be following a proper diet and training program consistently FIRST. Then, you can start implementing supplements as the sprinkles on top.&#10;&#10;Last, overtraining is real. I know you may have heard overtraining isn't a thing, or many of you may think you're not overtraining. The bottom line is more is not always better. When I was younger I would go to the gym for 2-3 hours - but I wasn't implementing proper rest times, and more so was focused on being physically at the gym. The key is to plan your rest days into your routine, 2 and 3 days are not the answer to getting in shape. You have to give your body the amount of rest time it needs to recover properly. I'd recommend hitting each muscle group no more than 2x a week to make sure you're getting enough rest.&#10;&#10;Hope you guys enjoyed this video! Be sure to click the thumbs up  below if you enjoyed this! Also, let me know down below what are some things you wish you knew when you started your fitness journey? I would love to hear your thoughts!&#10;&#10;See ya next video!&#10;&#10;4 Things YOU Should Know Before Working Out&#10;https://youtu.be/ub98tgcVAZM&#10;&#10;--------------------------------------------------------------------&#10;Check out My Supplement Stacks! &#10;&#10;For Fat Loss : http://vshred.fit/YTFatLossStack21&#10;&#10;For Muscle Building : http://vshred.fit/YTMuscleBuildingStack21&#10;--------------------------------------------------------------------&#10;&#10;Intro Song: Always Have by Castor Troy"
    channel_title "V Shred"
    channel_id "UCOFCwvhDoUvYcfpD7RJKQwA"
